\f1\b \cf0 Yes. This is technically feasible, and I actually think this version is substantially stronger than the \'93adaptive website framework\'94 version.
\f0\b0  You would be building something the user owns that sits between them and the existing web.\
The important architectural correction is that the 
\f1\b Mac app would not directly manipulate Chrome
\f0\b0 . The product would consist of a Mac app plus a Chrome extension:\

\f1\b Website \uc0\u8594  Chrome extension \u8594  local adaptation engine \u8594  modified website
\f0\b0 \
Chrome explicitly allows extensions to inject JavaScript and CSS into webpages, read their DOM, and change it. Chrome also officially supports 
\f1\b Native Messaging
\f0\b0 , which lets a Chrome extension communicate with a native macOS application.\
So your concept could genuinely work like this:\
