\f1\b \cf0 Finalist 2 \'97 Adaptive Web
\f0\b0 \
The idea is to make accessibility belong to the user rather than each individual website. Today, every website gives everyone essentially the same interface, even though an older user, someone with low vision, someone with reduced dexterity, or someone who gets overwhelmed by dense interfaces may need a completely different experience. Our system would automatically change how existing websites are presented to that person while keeping the underlying content and functionality the same. The broader thesis is: responsive design adapts to the screen; we want interfaces that adapt to the person.\
The product would most likely be a Chrome extension, potentially paired with a Mac app. The extension would inspect the structure of whatever website the user visits and then locally modify things like text size, spacing, button size, information density, navigation complexity, forms, explanations, and visual hierarchy. The user would initially create a functional accessibility profile based on what they struggle with, rather than being labeled by age or disability.\
The more interesting part is that it could also adapt based on behavior. If the system detects repeated errors, backtracking, hesitation, misclicks, or other signs that a page is causing difficulty, it could offer or automatically apply a more guided or simplified version of that interface. So adaptation comes from both what the user has told us about their needs and what is happening during the current interaction.\
Technically, we would build a constrained adaptation engine rather than letting AI freely regenerate websites. The extension would identify semantic elements like navigation, forms, primary actions, headings, and content sections, then choose from controlled transformations such as enlarge, simplify, reorder, collapse, explain, or guide step-by-step. AI could help understand the page and decide which transformations make sense, but the actual UI changes would come from a predefined component/rule system so the output stays reliable.\
For the final demo, we could open several real websites in Chrome using the same accessibility profile and show each one adapting differently based on its structure. Then we could change the profile or intentionally struggle with part of a site and show the interface responding in real time. The main research question would be whether these adaptations actually reduce errors, completion time, and difficulty compared with the normal static websites.\
