The idea is to make accessibility belong to the user rather than each individual website. Today, every website gives everyone essentially the same interface, even though an older user, someone with low vision, someone with reduced dexterity, or someone who gets overwhelmed by dense interfaces may need a completely different experience. Our system would automatically change how existing websites are presented to that person while keeping the underlying content and functionality the same. The broader thesis is: responsive design adapts to the screen; we want interfaces that adapt to the person.\
For the project, I\'92d definitely start with Chrome and only expand to other browsers if we have the time. The implementation would basically be a Chrome extension paired with a Mac app.\
The Chrome extension would be the core of the project because it would actually inspect and modify the websites the user visits. It could adjust things like information density, text and button sizing, navigation complexity, forms, hierarchy, explanations, etc. based on that user\'92s needs.\
The Mac app would act more like the user\'92s home base and the \'93brain\'94 behind it \'97 where they define their accessibility preferences, manage settings, control privacy, and potentially run a local LLM that helps the extension understand pages and decide which adaptations to make.\
I\'92d probably focus on getting the extension working first, since that proves the main idea, and then build the Mac app around it once the actual adaptive rendering works.\
