Thank you for getting back to me. At a high level, our project is focused on building a user-controlled accessibility layer for the web.\
The idea is that instead of relying on every individual website to accommodate different accessibility needs perfectly, a user would have their own system that can adapt existing websites to them. For example, the system could adjust information density, text and control sizing, navigation complexity, visual hierarchy, or guidance based on a user\'92s accessibility preferences and interaction needs, while preserving the underlying content and functionality of the website.\
For the semester, we are currently envisioning a Chrome extension as the core implementation. The extension would inspect the structure of a webpage and apply controlled interface adaptations in real time. We are also considering a companion desktop application that would manage the user\'92s accessibility profile and potentially run a local AI model to help interpret webpage structure and determine appropriate adaptations.\
A major technical focus would be making these adaptations reliable across different websites without allowing the system to alter or invent underlying information. We would also like to evaluate whether the adaptive interface improves measures such as task completion time, errors, and usability compared with the original website.\
We are still early in the ideation and scoping stage, so the exact implementation is flexible, and we would really value your feedback on what would make this a strong and appropriately scoped senior project.\
